Chapter: ProbeClosureCapture

 Finding 38's 2x2 has a confound, spotted in review: its two cells differ
 in HOW the closure is reached (directly vs by a self tail call) but also
 in WHAT the closure captures. `make-adder (n) = add3 n` captures a
 PARAMETER; `count-down`'s base case `add3 5` captures a LITERAL. The
 suspected mechanism turns on where a partial application's environment
 is allocated relative to the reused frame, so capture provenance is
 exactly the axis that should have been held fixed.

 This closes the grid. Two cells, both new:

   reached directly,  captures a literal   -- make-adder-lit, expected fine
   self tail call,    captures a parameter -- count-up, UNKNOWN

 A fault halts the program, so the expected-fine cell is driven first and
 the unknown second. `applied-lit 47` alone means count-up faulted and the
 self tail call is the discriminator, exactly as finding 38 claims. Both
 lines means capture provenance is the discriminator instead, and finding
 38's headline is wrong.

Section: Shapes

  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (x) (y) (z) = x + y + z

 Reached directly, but capturing a literal rather than its parameter.

  make-adder-lit : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  make-adder-lit (n) = add3 5

 Reached by a self tail call, but capturing a parameter rather than a
 literal. Counts UP so the base case closes over `n` and not a constant.

  count-up : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  count-up (n) = if n == 5 then add3 n else count-up (n + 1)

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("applied-lit " & show (((make-adder-lit 5) 20) 22))
    print-line-uni ("captured-param " & show (((count-up 0) 20) 22))
  end
